\title{GEMcluster: Semiparametric Elliptical Mixture Clustering}
\author{Long Feng and Dan Zhuang}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Overview}

\texttt{GEMcluster} implements a generalized EM algorithm for
high-dimensional semiparametric elliptical mixture clustering. The model uses
cluster-specific centers, a common sparse precision-shape matrix, and an
unknown common radial generator. This is useful when clusters are approximately
elliptical but may be heavy tailed.

The main algorithm has four blocks:

\begin{enumerate}
\item robust sparse K-median initialization;
\item posterior-responsibility updates using the estimated radial generator;
\item radial-score weighted center updates;
\item common-shape updates using a spatial-sign pilot, Tyler iteration, POET
regularization, and graphical lasso.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Fixed K}

The following example uses small tuning values to keep runtime short. For
simulation studies, increase the number of GEM iterations, initialization
starts, and outer starts.

<<fixed-k, eval=FALSE>>=
library(GEMcluster)

set.seed(1)
K <- 3
p <- 20
Mu <- dense_block_means(K = K, p = p, signal = 0.8)
Sigma <- make_ar_cov(p = p, rho = 0.5)
dat <- simulate_elliptical_mixture(
  n = 90,
  Mu = Mu,
  Sigma = Sigma,
  family = "t",
  t_df = 5,
  seed = 1
)

fit <- gem_fit(
  dat$X,
  K = K,
  max_outer = 5,
  init_n_boot = 2,
  init_nstart = 4,
  outer_nstart = 1
)

table(fit$cluster)
cluster_accuracy(fit$cluster, dat$label)$accuracy
@

\section{GAP-LSE selection of K}

\texttt{gem\_select\_k\_gap()} fits GEM on a candidate grid and compares the
observed radial dispersion with column-permuted reference samples. It returns
both the one-standard-error rule and the maximum-gap rule.

<<gap-k, eval=FALSE>>=
gap <- gem_select_k_gap(
  dat$X,
  K_grid = 2:5,
  B = 20,
  control = list(
    max_outer = 5,
    init_n_boot = 2,
    init_nstart = 4,
    outer_nstart = 1
  )
)

gap$best_K
gap$summary
@

